{
  "step_1": "The decision reason considers the minimum probability of each lottery and prefers the lottery with the higher minimum probability. For Lottery A, the minimum non-zero probability is 1% (for 0 Euros), but the smallest probability associated with a positive outcome is 33% (for 2500 Euros). However, the 'minimum probability' as a decision rule could be interpreted in different ways: (1) the smallest probability assigned to any outcome in the lottery, which is 1% for Lottery A (for 0 Euros), and 100% for Lottery B (since it has only one outcome). Under this interpretation, Lottery B has a higher minimum probability (100% > 1%), so the reason would prefer Lottery B. (2) Alternatively, if 'minimum probability' refers to the smallest probability assigned to a positive payoff, then Lottery A has 33% (for 2500 Euros) and Lottery B has 100%, so again Lottery B would be preferred. In both interpretations, the relevant information (probabilities of outcomes) is available from the problem, and the rule can be applied. Thus, the decision reason is applicable and leads to preferring Lottery B.",
